1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The passage is the classic patriotic-illusion paragraph of *El escritor argentino y la tradición*, The Argentine Writer and the Tradition, 1932, often reprinted under the heading *Patria*.

In the Spanish text Borges writes “las ilusiones del patriotismo no tienen fin” and illustrates the point with three historians / patriots writing in praise of their own countries, the Greek topographer/historian, the English Whig historian and the German philosopher of the *Reden an die deutsche Nation*:

* the Greek Pausanias/Pericles – the funeral-oraison / *Periegesis* praise of Hellas;
* Thomas Babington Macaulay, the English historian of England;
* Johann Gottlieb Fichte, the German author of the Addresses to the German Nation.

The names most commonly quoted in the English tradition for the three illustrations are Pericles, Thomas Babington Macaulay, Johann Gottlieb Fichte.

